## Authority this case is graded against -- and why this case is unusually well specified

* **X960 platform support TPS**, fetched 2026-10-20:

      TPS row 277 | Management | TACACS+ | Logging | Priority 1 | Supported: Yes

* **"TACACS+ AAA" TFS**, project 1599, section **"Logging specifications"**.  Unusually for
  this campaign, the feature under test is specified **line by line**, and the TFS opens the
  section by saying why:

  > There are no explicit logging requirements for this project. However, to help with
  > debugging customer issues, there are events that we will log.

  Seven specifications follow, and this case is a direct sweep of them:

  | spec | a log message will be generated when ... |
  | --- | --- |
  | S1599.1.080 | communication is **established** with a TACACS+ server |
  | S1599.1.081 | communication is **lost** with a TACACS+ server |
  | S1599.1.082 | login authentication **succeeds** |
  | S1599.1.083 | login authentication **fails** |
  | S1599.1.084 | `enable` authentication **succeeds** |
  | S1599.1.085 | `enable` authentication **fails** |
  | S1599.1.086 | accounting notification **fails** after every configured server has been tried |

* 🔥 **S1599.2.25 makes the log the specified interface, not a convenience.**  The TFS
  explicitly declines to provide a debugger: *"This functionality will not implement a
  'debug TACACS+' command as **log messages are considered sufficient**."*  So on this feature
  a missing log line is not a cosmetic gap -- it is the removal of the only diagnostic the
  design offers.  That is the standard this case grades against.
* **S1599.1.090** -- the existing `debug aaa accounting` / `authentication` is enhanced for
  this feature.  The TACACS+ Feature Page names the form: *debug AAA
  Accounting|Authentication|Authorization|All*.
* **S1599.1.001 - .004** also require that a failure to communicate with a server, and the
  exhaustion of all configured servers, are logged.

## Objective (designed by the tester)

Drive each of the seven specified events on the real DUT against the real server, and record
what the DUT's log actually says -- the exact text, facility and severity -- or record that it
says nothing.  Because S1599.2.25 removes the debugger in favour of these messages, "no
message" is a finding against a feature the TPS declares supported.

