## STEP 3 (designed) -- S1599.1.080: a log when communication is ESTABLISHED

    awplus(config)# tacacs-server host 10.38.22.33 key testing123
    awplus(config)# aaa accounting login default start-stop group tacacs+

**`<id>/swi_d.cfg` was saved at this point** (2026-10-20 14:41 NZDT).

Accounting rather than authentication is used as the stimulus here deliberately: it makes the
DUT talk to the server on every login **without** changing the authentication method list, so
this step carries none of the §7E.1 lockout risk.

    netlogin --proto ssh --user manager --password friend --cmd 'show clock'
      LOGIN RESULT: USER  proto=ssh  user=manager  seconds=0.3

**The exchange demonstrably happened** -- the server received both records:

    Oct 20 14:41:18 | 10.38.22.34 | manager | ssh | 10.38.22.33 | start | start_time=1792460465 | task_id=47 ...
    Oct 20 14:41:18 | 10.38.22.34 | manager | ssh | 10.38.22.33 | stop  | stop_time=1792460465  | task_id=47 ...

The DUT's log for the same window, IMISH echoes removed:

    2026 Oct 20 01:41:04 auth.notice awplus sshd-session[10420]: Connection from 10.38.22.33 port 55312 ...
    2026 Oct 20 01:41:05 auth.notice awplus sshd-session[10420]: Accepted keyboard-interactive/pam for manager ...
    2026 Oct 20 01:41:05 auth.notice awplus sshd-session[10423]: Session open for user manager ...
    2026 Oct 20 01:41:05 auth.notice awplus sshd-session[10420]: Session close for user manager ...

-- sshd lines only.  And across the **whole** log buffer:

    awplus# show log | include tacplus|tacacs|TACACS|TACPLUS
    2026 Oct 20 01:04:33 auth.notice awplus sshd-session[7602]: PAM-tacplus: User taclow is authenticated

still the single pre-existing line from STEP 2.

### 🟠 FINDING 1 -- S1599.1.080 is not implemented: a successful first exchange with a TACACS+ server is logged nowhere

Two round trips to the server completed successfully and were recorded by the server itself,
and the DUT produced **no log message of any kind** about establishing communication.
Confirmed again at STEP 5, where a server that had gone `Dead` came back to `Alive` and again
produced no "re-established" message.

The DUT does track the state -- `show tacacs+` carries a per-server `Status` column -- but
`show` is not the interface the TFS specifies, and a `show` cannot be sent to a syslog
collector, which is the "help with debugging customer issues" purpose the Logging
specifications section states.

Severity: 🟠.  The absence is most felt at the moment an operator needs it least (things are
working), but it also means there is no timestamped record of when a server came back, which
is exactly what a post-incident investigation asks for.  Against S1599.1.080, with TPS row 277
= Supported: Yes.

## STEP 4 (designed) -- S1599.1.086: a log when accounting notification fails after every server has been tried

The single configured server was replaced with an unreachable one on the same segment
(10.38.22.60, confirmed dead with `tools/tb ping tb277 eth3 10.38.22.60 2` -> 100% loss):

    awplus(config)# no tacacs-server host 10.38.22.33
    awplus(config)# tacacs-server host 10.38.22.60 key testing123

    netlogin --proto ssh --user manager --password friend --cmd 'show clock'
      LOGIN RESULT: USER  proto=ssh  user=manager  seconds=3.4        (0.3 s when the server is up)

    FILEWIN /var/log/tac_plus.acct ... new-bytes=0        <- the server received nothing

DUT log:

    2026 Oct 20 01:42:07 auth.notice  sshd-session[10527]: Accepted keyboard-interactive/pam for manager from 10.38.22.33 port 55314 ssh2
    2026 Oct 20 01:42:10 auth.err     sshd-session[10527]: tac_connect_single_vrf: connection failed with 10.38.22.60:49: Transport endpoint is not connected
    2026 Oct 20 01:42:10 auth.warning sshd-session[10527]: _pam_account: error sending start (fd)
    2026 Oct 20 01:42:14 auth.err     sshd-session[10527]: tac_connect_single_vrf: connection failed with 10.38.22.60:49: Transport endpoint is not connected
    2026 Oct 20 01:42:14 auth.warning sshd-session[10527]: _pam_account: error sending stop (fd)

**STEP 4 VERDICT: PASS -- S1599.1.086, and S1599.1.001/.004's logging half.**  The failure is
logged twice over, and better than the specification asks:

* the **transport** failure names the server, its port and the reason
  (`connection failed with 10.38.22.60:49: Transport endpoint is not connected`);
* the **accounting** failure is reported separately and **distinguishes the START from the
  STOP** (`error sending start (fd)` / `error sending stop (fd)`), so a reader can tell which
  record was lost;
* the login itself still succeeded -- accounting failure does not deny access -- which is
  correct and is visible in the same three lines.

Measured side effect: the failed accounting attempt costs **3.1 s of added login latency**
(3.4 s against 0.3 s).

## STEP 5 (designed) -- S1599.1.081: a log when communication is LOST

This one needs a server that **was** reachable and then is not, which is harder than it
sounds on this bench: the only working server is the box itself, directly attached on
`vlan10`, and stopping `tac_plus` is root (the Test Engineer's), while shutting port1.0.10 or
port1.0.48 is forbidden by the session.  Two methods were tried.

**Method A -- a blackhole route.  It does NOT work, and that is worth recording.**

    awplus(config)# ip route 10.38.22.33/32 null

    awplus# show ip route 10.38.22.33
    Routing entry for 10.38.22.33/32
      Known via "static", distance 1, metric 0
        is a summary, null

The route installs and the RIB shows it as the blackhole, **yet the DUT kept reaching the
server**: a console login immediately afterwards still delivered its accounting records
(`task_id=50`, `task_id=51`) and `show tacacs+` still read `Alive`.  A `/32` blackhole does
**not** override on-link delivery to a directly connected host for the DUT's own
control-plane traffic.  Removed again (`no ip route 10.38.22.33/32 null`).

**Method B -- a bogus static ARP entry.  This works.**

    awplus(config)# arp 10.38.22.33 0000.dead.beef

State **before** (established): `show tacacs+` -> `10.38.22.33  Alive`, console login 0.4 s,
accounting records arriving (`task_id=48`, `49`).

State **after**, driven with a console login (the console is unaffected by the broken ARP;
SSH from the box is, which is why this leg used the console):

    LOGIN RESULT: USER  user=manager  seconds=5.4            (0.4 s before)
    FILEWIN /var/log/tac_plus.acct ... new-bytes=0           (the server received nothing)

    awplus# show tacacs+
    10.38.22.33                                   Dead       <- was `Alive`

    2026 Oct 20 01:45:55 authpriv.warning awplus login: _pam_account: error sending stop (fd)
    2026 Oct 20 01:46:02 authpriv.warning awplus login: _pam_account: error sending start (fd)

Then restored (`no arp 10.38.22.33` -- note the negation takes **no MAC**; `no arp 10.38.22.33
0000.dead.beef` is rejected with `% Invalid input detected`), and recovery verified:

    LOGIN RESULT: USER  user=manager  seconds=0.4
    FILEWIN ... new-lines=4        (task_id 51 stop, 52 start/stop -- records flowing again)
    awplus# show tacacs+  ->  10.38.22.33   Alive

**STEP 5 VERDICT: PARTIAL -- the loss is detected and recorded, but not as a log message.**

* ✅ **`show tacacs+` distinguishes the two failure modes.**  A server that was never reached
  reads **`Error`** (measured in T33411 for 10.38.22.60); a server that was `Alive` and then
  became unreachable reads **`Dead`**.  With `Unknown` for a freshly configured server, that
  is **four** distinct states, and the DUT genuinely knows that communication was lost.
* ❌ **No log message says so.**  The only lines produced are
  `_pam_account: error sending start/stop (fd)` -- the *accounting* failure of S1599.1.086,
  not a statement about the server.  Notably the `tac_connect_single_vrf: connection failed`
  line of STEP 4 is **absent** here, because this failure is a TCP timeout rather than an
  immediate connect error, so the loss case is logged **less** than the never-established
  case.
* ❌ **Nothing at all is logged when communication comes back** (STEP 3's FINDING 1 again).

### 🟠 FINDING 2 -- S1599.1.081 is satisfied only by a `show` command, not by a log message

The device detects loss of communication precisely enough to move a server from `Alive` to
`Dead`, and emits no log line to that effect.  An operator watching syslog sees only that an
accounting record could not be sent; they cannot tell from the log that a server has gone
away, and they get no notification at all when it returns.  Against S1599.1.081 (and
S1599.1.080 for the return), with S1599.2.25 removing the debugger that might otherwise cover
for it.

### ⚠️ A third caveat on the `platforms/x960.md` §7E.2 `Last login:` discriminator

Noticed by the sentinel watching this leg and confirmed against the box-side transcript
`/tmp/cktac/console-u2.log`.  While the accounting server was black-holed, a single console
login printed the banner line **twice**:

    awplus login: manager
    Last login: Tue Oct 20 01:45:50 UTC 2026 on ttyS0
    Last login: Tue Oct 20 01:45:57 UTC 2026 on ttyS0
    AlliedWare Plus (TM) 0.0.0 10/12/26 06:38:00

-- one `login:` prompt, one username echo, **two** `Last login:` lines about 7 s apart, one
banner.  A login outside the window prints exactly one:

    awplus login: manager
    Last login: Tue Oct 20 01:48:22 UTC 2026 on ttyS0
    AlliedWare Plus (TM) 0.0.0 10/12/26 06:38:00

Measured honestly: across the black-holed window there were **4 logins and 6 `Last login:`
lines**, so two of the four doubled; every login before and after the window produced exactly
one.  The ~7 s gap matches the accounting timeout.  **The correlation with the unreachable
server is clear; the mechanism is not isolated**, and this case did not establish why two of
four doubled rather than all four.  Stated as an observation, not a rule.

⇒ `platforms/x960.md` §7E.2 now needs **three** caveats before anyone uses `Last login:` as a
discriminator: it is absent for a login with no local account (T33411 STEP 13, confirming the
original rule), it can carry a **deleted user's** timestamp when the UID is recycled (T33411's
`ckalt` finding), and it can be **duplicated** when the login waited on an unreachable AAA
server (here).

## STEP 6 (designed) -- S1599.1.082: a log when login authentication SUCCEEDS

This is the first step that needs the authentication method list, so the escape route of
T33411 STEP 4 was rebuilt and proven first, before the method list was touched:

    awplus(config)# username tacuser privilege 15 password localonly1
    conlogin --user tacuser --password localonly1 --expect user ...   -> USER, then `awplus#`

`tacuser`'s LOCAL password (`localonly1`) differs from its TACACS+ password (`testing123`),
which makes STEP 7 a discriminator as well as a control, and `aaa authentication enable
default local` is left alone so `enable` always works.  Only then:

    awplus(config)# aaa authentication login default group tacacs+ local

    conlogin --user tacuser --password testing123 --expect user
        --restore-user tacuser --restore-password testing123
      LOGIN RESULT: USER  user=tacuser  seconds=0.6  prompt='awplus>'

DUT log (`show log | include tacplus|FAILED LOGIN|aaa-configure`, IMISH echoes removed):

    2026 Oct 20 01:50:14 authpriv.notice awplus login: PAM-tacplus: User tacuser is authenticated
    2026 Oct 20 01:50:14 user.notice     awplus IMISH[11579]: [SCRIPT]aaa-configure tacuser enable-mode 2 cmd-acct-priv 0 update-intval 0
    2026 Oct 20 01:50:16 authpriv.notice awplus login: PAM-tacplus: User tacuser is authenticated
    2026 Oct 20 01:50:16 user.notice     awplus IMISH[11624]: [SCRIPT]aaa-configure tacuser enable-mode 2 cmd-acct-priv 0 update-intval 0

**STEP 6 VERDICT: PASS -- S1599.1.082.**  Exactly one
`PAM-tacplus: User <name> is authenticated` line per successful login (two logins, two lines),
naming the user, at `authpriv.notice`.

A second line is worth keeping even though no spec asks for it: **`[SCRIPT]aaa-configure
<user> enable-mode <n> cmd-acct-priv <n> update-intval <n>`** is emitted at every login and
records the AAA profile the session was given.  It is a useful correlation anchor -- it names
the user, it appears once per login, and `cmd-acct-priv 0` / `update-intval 0` state the
command-accounting and periodic-update settings in force for that session.

## STEP 7 (designed) -- S1599.1.083: a log when login authentication FAILS

Same configuration; the same account offering the password that exists only in the LOCAL
database, which §7E.1 says will be refused because the server answers Reject:

    conlogin --user tacuser --password localonly1 --expect fail
        --restore-user tacuser --restore-password testing123
      LOGIN RESULT: FAIL  user=tacuser  seconds=1.6  prompt='Login incorrect'

DUT log:

    2026 Oct 20 01:51:10 authpriv.err    awplus login: PAM-tacplus: auth failed: 2
    2026 Oct 20 01:51:11 authpriv.notice awplus login: FAILED LOGIN SESSION FROM ttyS0 FOR tacuser, Permission denied
    2026 Oct 20 01:51:18 authpriv.notice awplus login: PAM-tacplus: User tacuser is authenticated   <- the restore login

**STEP 7 VERDICT: PASS -- S1599.1.083.**  A failed login produces **two** lines and between
them they carry everything an investigation needs:

* the **protocol-level** cause at `authpriv.err` -- `auth failed: 2`, where 2 is the TACACS+
  `AUTHEN_STATUS_FAIL` code, so the log distinguishes a server Reject from a transport
  problem (STEP 4's `connection failed`) and from a key problem (T33411's
  `inconsistent reply body, incorrect key?`);
* the **session-level** record at `authpriv.notice` -- `FAILED LOGIN SESSION FROM ttyS0 FOR
  tacuser, Permission denied`, naming the line and the user.

The restore login's success line immediately afterwards shows the two outcomes are
unambiguous against each other in a single stream.

## STEP 8 -- the facility and severity inventory

Collected across this case and T33411, because a log message is only useful if a syslog
collector can be told how to select it.  This is what a filter for TACACS+ events on this
build has to match.

| event | facility.severity | program | text |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| login authentication succeeded (console) | `authpriv.notice` | `login` | `PAM-tacplus: User <u> is authenticated` |
| login authentication succeeded (VTY) | `auth.notice` | `sshd-session[pid]` | `PAM-tacplus: User <u> is authenticated` |
| login authentication failed, server Reject | `authpriv.err` | `login` | `PAM-tacplus: auth failed: 2` |
| login refused, session level | `authpriv.notice` | `login` | `FAILED LOGIN SESSION FROM <line> FOR <u>, Permission denied` |
| server unreachable (connect fails at once) | `auth.err` | `sshd-session[pid]` | `tac_connect_single_vrf: connection failed with <ip>:49: Transport endpoint is not connected` |
| that server was the n-th of n | `auth.err` | `sshd-session[pid]` | `PAM-tacplus: connection failed srv <index>: ...` |
| all servers exhausted | `auth.err` | `sshd-session[pid]` | `PAM-tacplus: no more servers to connect` |
| authentication abandoned after exhaustion | `auth.err` | `sshd-session[pid]` | `PAM-tacplus: user not authenticated by TACACS+` |
| wrong shared key | `auth.err` | `sshd-session[pid]` | `tac_authen_read_timeout: inconsistent reply body, incorrect key?` |
| accounting record could not be sent (VTY) | `auth.warning` | `sshd-session[pid]` | `_pam_account: error sending start\|stop (fd)` |
| accounting record could not be sent (console) | `authpriv.warning` | `login` | `_pam_account: error sending start\|stop (fd)` |
| the AAA profile applied at login | `user.notice` | `IMISH[pid]` | `[SCRIPT]aaa-configure <u> enable-mode <n> cmd-acct-priv <n> update-intval <n>` |

Two practical observations:

1. 🔥 **There is no single facility, severity or keyword that selects all TACACS+ events.**
   The same event is `authpriv.*` from the console and `auth.*` from a VTY, the text prefix is
   `PAM-tacplus:` for some lines, `tac_connect_single_vrf:` / `tac_authen_read_timeout:` for
   others and `_pam_account:` for accounting, and severity ranges from `notice` to `err`
   **for outcomes of the same kind**.  A collector filter has to be written as a union of
   patterns.  Worth saying out loud because S1599.2.25 makes these messages the whole
   diagnostic surface.
2. **Severity is not a fault indicator here.**  Every `auth.err` line in this case is the DUT
   correctly reporting a control the tester created on purpose (logged-output.md §2).

## STEP 9 (designed) -- removal: the log goes quiet again

    awplus(config)# aaa authentication login default local      (restored first; `manager`
                                                                 then logs in locally, 0.4 s)
    awplus(config)# no username tacuser
    awplus(config)# no aaa accounting login default
    awplus(config)# no tacacs-server host 10.38.22.33

    awplus# show tacacs+
    No TACACS+ servers currently defined

Under a 35 s capture, with one console login/logout and one full VTY session:

    12 packets captured
    FILEWIN /var/log/tac_plus.acct ... new-lines=1
    Oct 20 14:52:37 | manager | ttyS0 | unknown | stop | stop_time=1792461143 | task_id=60 ... elapsed_time=38

    tcpflow: 12 packet(s) total, 1 TCP flow(s)
      10.38.22.34:60842 -> 10.38.22.33:49  payload 165 B   <- the STOP record's exact signature
      10.38.22.33:49 -> 10.38.22.34:60842  payload  17 B

**That residual record is correct, not a leak.**  It is the STOP for the console session that
had logged in *while* accounting was still configured -- **S1599.1.026 again**: a session
retains the configuration it had at login, so its STOP is still sent after the configuration
is removed.  The payload (165 B out / 17 B in) is byte-identical to the STOP signature
measured in T33410 STEP 3, which is what identifies it.

A second, clean capture over another console login/logout -- with no session predating the
removal:

    0 packets captured
    FILEWIN /var/log/tac_plus.acct ... new-bytes=0 new-lines=0

and no new TACACS+ line of any kind in the DUT log.

**STEP 9 VERDICT: PASS.**  Removal is complete on all three instruments, and the one record
that did arrive is explained and attributable.

